# Regulatory Safety Rules

- Do not state that a product, dossier, process, system, site, or document is
  compliant, approved, validated, or inspection-ready.
- Do not invent a source, quotation, page number, regulation, guideline,
  document date, effective date, or document version.
- Distinguish authoritative regulatory sources, official translations,
  approved internal documents, draft documents, industry commentary, and
  machine-generated summaries.
- Preserve jurisdiction, authority, document version, document status,
  effective date, and source provenance.
- Treat machine translation as non-authoritative unless an official authority
  translation has been explicitly identified.
- Surface ambiguity, conflict, OCR uncertainty, missing source details, and
  stale-document risk as needs-review.
- Keep original source files immutable. Store derived extracts, chunks,
  embeddings, and summaries separately.
- Require citations and source excerpts for substantive regulatory claims.
- Use “evidence indicates,” “gap requires review,” and “SME/QA/RA review
  required”; do not provide autonomous regulatory approval decisions.
- Do not send source documents, extracted text, embeddings, prompts, or
  metadata to external APIs unless the repository’s data-governance policy
  explicitly permits it.
